[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Graceful Degradation and Shedding Load in System Design"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In distributed systems, handling traffic spikes and component failures gracefully is critical to maintaining high availability. When a system reaches its capacity limits, two vital strategies come into play: Graceful Degradation and Load Shedding."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Graceful Degradation"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Graceful degradation is the ability of a system to maintain core functionality even when some of its subsystems or dependencies fail or become overloaded. Instead of throwing a complete system error (like a 500 Internal Server Error), the system drops non-essential features."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Disabling personalized recommendations on an e-commerce homepage during high traffic."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Serving cached, slightly stale data when the primary database is experiencing high latency."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Hiding user avatars or high-resolution images to save bandwidth and processing power."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Load Shedding"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"When degradation is not enough and traffic exceeds absolute capacity, the system must actively drop requests to protect itself from cascading failures and total crashes. This is known as load shedding."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Rejecting non-critical background job requests to preserve resources for user-facing API calls."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Returning immediate HTTP 429 (Too Many Requests) or 503 (Service Unavailable) responses at the API gateway level."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Using random early detection (RED) or token bucket algorithms to limit incoming connections."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementation Example: Load Shedding Middleware"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Below is a conceptual Node.js Express middleware that demonstrates simple load shedding based on active concurrent requests."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"const MAX_CONCURRENT_REQUESTS = 1000;
let activeRequests = 0;

function loadSheddingMiddleware(req, res, next) {
  if (activeRequests >= MAX_CONCURRENT_REQUESTS) {
    res.status(503).json({
      error: "Service is temporarily overloaded. Please try again later."
    });
    return;
  }

  activeRequests++;
  res.on('finish', () => {
    activeRequests--;
  });

  next();
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Resilient Architectures"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Establish clear metrics and thresholds (CPU utilization, memory limits, queue length) that trigger shedding."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Prioritize traffic based on business value (e.g., checkout flows take precedence over analytics tracking)."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Combine load shedding with circuit breakers and timeouts to isolate failing downstream services."}]
